1. Visual Identity & Theme

Core Aesthetic: "Premium Stationery." The UI must feel like high-quality physical cardstock. Use --cream (#FAFAF7) as the base.

Texture: Always maintain a subtle SVG noise filter (opacity 0.02) over the background to kill the "flat" digital look.

Glassmorphism: Use backdrop-filter: contrast(95%) brightness(105%) blur(10px) on all major dashboard cards.

Borders: Avoid heavy strokes. Use 1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05) for a delicate, paper-like edge.

2. Typography Rules

Human/Emotional Moments: Use Instrument Serif for greetings, "Daily Memo" headers, and mission statements.

Functional/Data Moments: Use DM Sans for all buttons, navigation, tickers, and labels.

Numerical Precision: ALL financial figures and percentages MUST use font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; to prevent layout shift during updates.

3. Color Strategy (The "Calm" Palette)

Positive (Up): Mint/Emerald (#2D9A7E). Avoid bright "neon" greens.

Negative (Down): Terracotta/Muted Clay (#C87B5B). Avoid "Emergency Red."

Dark Mode: Use "Midnight Ink" (#1A1A1D) instead of pure black to maintain the stationery feel.

4. Motion & Interaction

Easing: Use Spring Physics exclusively: cubic-bezier(0.175, 0.885, 0.32, 1.275).

The "Tilt" Effect: Interactive cards should have a subtle 3D lift and shadow deepening on hover (translateY(-4px)).

Loading: Use "Shimmer" skeleton loaders for all data points and charts to simulate a premium, "thinking" application.

5. Architectural Constraints

Separation: Keep the Landing Page (marketing/storytelling) logic separate from the Dashboard (application) logic.

Sidebar: The Dashboard should always utilize a vertical sidebar containing contextual stats (e.g., "Top Mover").

Mission: Always keep a "Mission Moment" quote at the bottom of the sidebar to reinforce the Tuskegee/First-Gen identity.